using deep chain we can perform in  silico side directed mutagenesis on a  protein complex where we select the  position we want to mutate and the amino  acid we want to replace this lets us see  how the modification will affect our  complex on several parameters including  stability and binding  our example looks at the complex formed  by prolactin and its  receptor to input our pdb structure  click on data source in our case we will  be using the pdbid3mzg  our structure is displayed in three  dimensions  the orange color represents the receptor  and the green the prolactin  in the upper tab our sequence is  displayed with both chains in the  receptor sequence we will look for the  position  188. which contains histidine and select  it the letter will be highlighted as  well as the position in the structure  window at the same time in the site  directed mutagenesis window will appear  h1a  underscore what we will do is replace it  with r symbol of the arginine amino acid  and then click run experiment a window  pops up allowing us to select the app  and the scorer that we will use for the  experiment  we will select the log likelihood app in  both  and click on run  as we can see our experiment is launched  and it will take a few hours to process  once our experiment succeeds we can  explore the results  to do so we click on the experiment  within the results we see different  sections  we have options a table and a graph  in the options section we select what  parameters we want in the results table  and in the graph we'll select stability  and binding as our parameters  the results are presented as free  energies for both binding and stability  for a better binding or a higher  stability we're looking for the lowest  value  in the graph we will see that the native  structure represented with the pink dot  has a better binding and a higher  stability as expected  we can also compare result values in the  table  the experiment shows us that by  substituting the histidine and position  188 of the receptor with arginine will  reduce the binding between prolactin and  its receptor what we have done in just a  few clicks replicates what was done  experimentally in a research paper  highlighting how a single mutation on a  prolactin receptor can lead to a disease  called hyperprolactinemia  which causes infertility after seeing  how easy it is to run a site-directed  mutagenesis experiment in silico using  deep chain and the reliable results it  provides just think of all the  possibilities this powerful time and  resource saving tool offers  to learn more sign up now at  deepchain.bio thank you for listening  thank you for your time
 

